/* ============================================================================
   DIBSEN Island Plates — the islands themselves, photographed.

   These replace the procedurally sculpted karst. That decision was not taken
   lightly, and the reason is in the record: the sculpt went through twelve
   documented iterations — too short, notch submerged, "vase", "broccoli",
   "green-crowned boulder", faceted foliage, treeline too tidy, too bald, too
   green, "table legs" — and never arrived. The method was the ceiling: karst is
   the product of dissolution chemistry over millions of years, and a radius
   profile plus noise does not reproduce that. A better reference does not fix a
   method; it only makes the gap easier to see.

   So the islands are now keyed cutouts of real rock, drawn as camera-facing
   quads in real 3D space. What that keeps:
     · they still stand in the world, so they scale and occlude correctly
     · they are still raycast, so hover and click still work (alpha-tested, so
       only the rock is clickable, not its transparent corners)
     · scene fog is applied to them by hand, so they still sit in the air
   What it costs: they are flat. That is paid for by the camera never passing
   far enough around one to reveal it — see the bearing note in archipelago.js.

   Keyed with a plain luminance matte against the plates' black background. The
   waterline of each — where the rock's foot meets its own pool — was read off
   by eye rather than detected: four attempts at an automatic detector each
   broke on a different plate, and three assets do not need a rule that
   generalises to a population of three.
   ========================================================================== */
